{
  "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
  "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Robert C. Owen",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When this Court granted review in mid October in these consolidated cases, the cases exemplified the Fifth Circuit's settled approach to reviewing claims of error under this Court's 1989 decision in Penry v. Lynaugh. In both cases the court below failed to take seriously the requirement that capital jurors have a meaningful basis for giving effect to the relevant mitigating qualities of a defendant's evidence, and in both cases the court below found as a factual matter, both against common sense and this Court's holdings, that reasonable jurors would regard evidence that a defendant had experienced significant mistreatment or abuse as a child or had mental impairments as an adult as reasons to find him less dangerous rather than more dangerous."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Why, when we are told that, that the State will surely challenge Nelson in this Court, and we already have the issue before us, so all that you would achieve is delay, just substituting the Nelson case for this one?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robert C. Owen",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But because the jurors were never asked whether the mitigating evidence reduced the Defendant's culpability in such a way as to call for a life sentence, the resulting death sentences are unreliable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: So we're, we're asking whether this State court made an unreasonable decision at the time, and at the time regardless of what the Fifth Circuit has now said, at the time under Johnson, and -- and there is another earlier case, we said that you didn't have to give full mitigating effects; as long as there was some manner in which mitigating effect could be given that was enough."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "American Academy of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry and Child Welfare League of America",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "amici before the court, both the American Academy of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry, on the one hand, the Child Welfare League of America, on the other, their amicus briefs I think really -- really detailed the fact that this is a commonplace understanding in our society."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Edward L. Marshall",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When the State court considered these Penry claims in 1994, 1999 and January 2001, this Court's decisions in Graham and Johnson made it clear that the Eighth Amendment requires only that a jury be only able to consider mitigating evidence in some manner, not in every conceivable manner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What other choice does defense counsel have, given that the jury is going to get a question, is this man likely to be a danger in the future? What else could counsel argue?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Marshall",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Reasonable likelihood that the juror was precluded from considering the relevant mitigating evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What's my answer under Texas law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Marshall",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "court considering this case was looking at Graham, in which the Court Stated that that evidence fit within future dangerousness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And what's the Texas case that says that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "arguments that counsel made?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Marshall",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "there are 47 inmates on Texas death row that were sentenced under this statute that remain there. There are nine cases which have litigated Penry claims all the way to conclusion in Federal court. There are 25 more that are somewhere in the pipeline either in State court or Federal court. I've actually looked at the cases and 17 of those cases, 17 of the 34 that are still in the system, have evidence that's almost identical to these cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Owen",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First is to remind the Court that Graham was a Teague case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Owen",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0009",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Brewer was hit numerous times. That's his mom's word. Hit with objects only twice, but hit numerous times."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Owen",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0011",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There was no mercy option. There was no mitigation instruction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Owen",
      "case_id": "2005-05-11284",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0012",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "habeas relief in Mr. Cole's case."
    },
